{
  "id": "Compound::DB06777 - Disease::DOID:12236",
  "verbalization": "Chenodeoxycholic Acid appears to be linked to Primary Biliary Cirrhosis through a chain of biological relationships involving bile acids and their derivatives. The evidence suggests that Chenodeoxycholic Acid, through its resemblance to Tauroursodeoxycholic Acid and Ursodeoxycholic Acid, shares a common pathway with bile acids, which are known to influence liver function and bile secretion. Ursodeoxycholic Acid, in turn, is associated with the treatment of Primary Biliary Cirrhosis, indicating a potential therapeutic role. The recurring theme of bile acid metabolism and its impact on liver disease supports the hypothesis that Chenodeoxycholic Acid may have a similar effect. The integration of these relationships highlights a possible mechanism by which bile acid derivatives, including Chenodeoxycholic Acid, could contribute to the pathogenesis of Primary Biliary Cirrhosis.",
